ndard format specifiers like %Y for year, %m for month, %d for day, and so on to Go's reference time notation.\n\nContinuing with the remaining format specifiers and noting which ones need special handling...\n\nNow I'm realizing the best approach is to scan through the format string, map each % sequence to either a Go layout token or a sentinel placeholder, then do a post-processing pass to calculate values like ISO week numbers, century, and space-padded hours that Go's time.Format doesn't handle directly. I see the issue now—the function needs to handle directives that can't be represented as Go layout tokens (like %s for unix epoch, %j for day of year, %u/%w for weekday), so I'll need sentinels for those too and replace them after formatting. A cleaner approach would be to have `strftimeToGoLayout` convert the format string and mark unsupported directives with sentinels, then post-process the formatted result to substitute the actual values.\n\nFor the -i and -s modes with TZ=GMT, I need to compute the time delta as a duration, convert it to seconds, and construct a time.Time from the epoch plus that delta, then format it with a fixed GMT timezone.\n\nImplementing the conversion logic...\n\nFor -r mode, I'm setting up regex patterns to parse different relative time formats like syslog timestamps, ISO-8601, RFC 2822, and lastlog, then extracting the date components to calculate the relative age. For the output formatting, I need to show the two largest non-zero time units with \"ago\" appended, like \"2h15m ago\" or \"45s ago\".\n\nNow I'm reviewing the core design decisions and requirements to make sure my implementation covers all the modes and flags properly, then I'll write out the complete code structure.\n\nI need to be careful with the flag package behavior—when it encounters an unknown flag, it exits with code 2 and prints to stderr, which should satisfy the test requirement for a non-zero exit with usage information in stderr.\n\nLooking at the test case