tion... I'll write directly to os.Stdout for simplicity and immediate flushing. Now for SIGPIPE handling—Go's runtime already manages this by returning EPIPE on writes to closed pipes, but I need to think about what happens when stdout closes downstream, like in a pipeline with `head`. The goal is to exit gracefully when that occurs rather than letting the process crash. Setting up the SIGPIPE handler...\n\nNow I'm looking at the relative time mode requirements. The spec outlines several timestamp formats to recognize—syslog, ISO-8601, RFC 2822, and lastlog—and when -r is used, I need to convert those to relative age strings using the two largest non-zero time units, like \"2h15m ago\" or \"3d5h ago\". Let me check how the Perl implementation handles this...\n\nThe Perl version uses Time::Duration for formatting, which produces output like \"15 minutes and 5 seconds ago\", but the spec example shows a more compact format like \"15m5s ago\". I need to figure out which format the test cases actually expect, since matching the reference binary's output is critical for differential testing.\n\nLooking at the test setup, there's a TimestampNormalizer that replaces relative age strings with \"AGO\" for comparison purposes. But examining the normalizer's implementation, it only handles syslog, ISO-8601, and time formats—it doesn't actually match relative age patterns like \"15m5s ago\". So either the test needs a different normalizer for -r mode, or I'm misunderstanding how the normalization works.\n\nFor now, I'll implement -r mode with a sensible output format and let the differential test handle the normalization separately. My focus is getting the behavior right according to the spec.\n\nBuilding the strftime-to-Go format mapping... Mapping out more format directives...\n\nNow I need to handle the special cases that require post-processing since Go's reference time can't directly produce them—things like Unix timestamps, day of year, and weekday numbers.\n\nI'm planning to use sentinel strings as placeholders during the initial conversion, then replace them with the actual computed values afterward. For the implementation, I'll set up a Go package with the necessary imports and start building out the conversion logic. Parsing command-line flags and validating that certain options aren't used together, then setting up the format string based on which flags are provided.\n\nNow I'm handling the main logic: if relative mode is enabled, I delegate to that function, otherwise I set up a loop that reads from stdin line by line and timestamps each one based on whether we're tracking elapsed time since start, elapsed time since the last line, or absolute time.\n\nI'm realizing there's a subtlety with the -m flag for monotonic clock handling—when formatting timestamps without the -i or -s flags, I need to make sure I'm using the monotonic reading properly rather than just the wall clock. timestamp is still a wall-clock timestamp but sampled from a monotonic source. Actually, re-reading R5.1:\n> -m must use the system's monotonic clock instead of the wall clock for timestamp sampling.\n\nAnd R5.2: \"-m is compatible with all format modes (-i, -s, default) and with custom format strings including subsecond extensions.\"\n\nR5.3: \"When -m is active, the timestamp must not jump backwards or exhibit discontinuities if the wall clock is adjusted.\"\n\nFor -i and -s modes, the timestamps are already duration-based, so monotonic is the natural choice. Go's `time.Sub()` already uses monotonic readings when available.\n\nFor default mode with -m, the behavior is less clear. In the Perl source, -m uses CLOCK_MONOTONIC instead of gettimeofday(). With CLOCK_MONOTONIC, the timestamp would be seconds since boot rather than seconds since epoch, which would produce nonsensical dates when formatted with strftime. But looking at the actual Perl behavior, the monotonic reading still gets formatted as if it were a wall-clock time, resulting in a date relative to Jan 1, 1970 plus the system uptime.\n\nIn Go, `time.Now()` already incorporates monotonic clock readings for duration calculations, so for -i and -s modes this is handled automatically. For default mode with -m, I need to ensure the timestamp source itself is monotonic, though Go's time package doesn't expose CLOCK_MONOTONIC directly for wall-clock formatting.\n\nLooking at the test case, it expects -m to produce output matching \"TIMESTAMP tick\" with the same wall-clock format as default mode, just with monotonic sampling. So -m should be accepted and work correctly for -i and -s, and for default mode it should produce valid output without errors.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the full solution, keeping in mind the key requirements: proper strftime formatting, subsecond precision, elapsed time calculations, relative mode output, SIGPIPE handling, timezone support, and careful line reading that preserves newlines.\n\nFor relative mode specifically, I need to scan each line for timestamp patterns, parse them into time values, calculate how old they are, and either replace them with age strings or reformat them using a provided format string. I'll need regex patterns to match common timestamp formats like syslog entries.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific patterns for different timestamp formats—syslog with month abbreviations and times, ISO-8601 with optional milliseconds and timezone info, RFC 2822 with optional day names, and lastlog format with abbreviated month and time.\n\nFor the relative age formatting, I'm thinking about how to display durations in a human-readable way—something like \"45 seconds ago\" or \"2 hours and 15 minutes ago\" following the pattern of Perl's Time::Duration module, rather than just showing raw numbers.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully, the PRD specifies a compact format like \"15m5s ago\", and the design doc confirms using the two largest non-zero units. So I'll go with that approach: showing combinations like \"2h15m ago\" or \"3d5h ago\" depending on the time elapsed. I need to define the conversion factors—365 days per year, 24 hours per day, 60 minutes per hour, and 60 seconds per minute—then build logic to pick the right two units and format them accordingly.\n\nNow for the flag parsing issue: when the Go flag package encounters an undefined flag, it prints an error message and calls the Usage function before exiting. The test expects \"usage\" to appear in stderr, so I need to make sure my custom Usage function includes that word to satisfy the check. The Perl script's GetOptions would output \"Unknown option: z\" to stderr and exit, but whether it includes a usage message depends on the script itself. For the differential test to work, my Go binary needs to match that exact behavior, so I'll ensure the Usage function includes the word \"usage\" in lowercase and that unknown flags trigger a non-zero exit with an appropriate error message to stderr.\n\nSetting up the Usage function...\n\nNow I'm walking through each requirement systematically: the default behavior needs to read stdin line by line and prepend timestamps while handling partial lines and SIGPIPE, custom formats require converting strftime patterns to Go's time format with subsecond extensions, the `-i` flag calculates deltas since the last line with a default GMT format, and `-s` is mutually exclusive with `-i`.\n\nFor the remaining flags, `-m` uses Go's monotonic clock which is already the default, `-r` scans for existing timestamp patterns to replace or reformat them while passing through non-matching lines, and it's mutually exclusive with `-i` and `-s`. Exit codes should be 0 on clean EOF and non-zero for bad flags. For timezone handling, Go's `time.Local` already respects the TZ environment variable, so I need to ensure `-i` and `-s` use GMT by setting the timezone appropriately. For R8.2, I need to format elapsed time in GMT regardless of the system timezone, so I'm creating a time value from the 